              "id": "56ad1f15-c9f1-3aed-aa41-bd9e8709ac98",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to undefined behavior or security vulnerabilities. In this case, the function `std::getenv` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it accesses the environment variables, which can be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` and other unsafe C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library or other trusted libraries. Validate all input and output data. Be especially careful with data that comes from an external source or user input. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv_s` function, which is safer because it checks the size of the buffer before writing to it. Here is how you can modify the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t requiredSize;\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, nullptr, 0, \"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    return sizeof(T) * size_W;\n}\nelse\n{\n    char* value = new char[requiredSize];\n    getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\n    // Use the value\n    delete[] value;\n    return size_W;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `std::getenv`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible. Always check the validity of the links before using them."
