            {
              "id": "00de5619-9b6f-3d28-b92e-8d787e423f75",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it returns a pointer to a string that represents the value of the environment variable. This string can be modified by another process, leading to potential race conditions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `std::getenv` and instead use safer alternatives that do not have the same potential for race conditions or other security issues. Also, ensure that your code does not rely on the value of an environment variable for its security.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you could use the `std::getenv_s` function, which is a safer version of `std::getenv`. Here's how you could modify the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t requiredSize;\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    sz = sizeof(float) * lwork;\n}\nelse\n{\n    char* value = new char[requiredSize];\n    getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\n    sz = lwork;\n    delete[] value;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `std::getenv`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6ddd9e12-a971-3744-b678-68237579daed",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities. In this case, the function `std::getenv` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it accesses the environment variables directly and its return value can be modified by other threads, leading to race conditions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function directly. Instead, use safer alternatives that are thread-safe and do not allow for buffer overflows or other types of vulnerabilities. Always validate and sanitize the input and output of your functions. Be aware of the potential for race conditions and other concurrency issues in your code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv`, consider using a safer alternative like `std::getenv_s` or `std::secure_getenv` if available. If these functions are not available, you can use a mutex to ensure thread safety when accessing environment variables.\n\n```cpp\n#include <mutex>\nstd::mutex mtx;\nmtx.lock();\nchar* buffer = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nmtx.unlock();\nif (buffer == nullptr) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>` for `std::getenv`\n- `<mutex>` for `std::mutex`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Code Review Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-code-review-guide/)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n- [CWE-362: Concurrent Execution using Shared Resource with Improper Synchronization ('Race Condition')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/362.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24a933bb-6690-3fc2-bc96-b51098f5b985",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that represents the value for the specified environment variable. The problem is that the returned string can be modified by subsequent calls to `std::getenv`, `std::setenv`, or `std::unsetenv`, and this can lead to race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` in multithreaded applications. If you need to use environment variables, consider copying the values into your own buffers to avoid race conditions. Also, always check the returned pointer from `std::getenv` for null before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (buffer_size != nullptr) {\n    sz = lwork;\n} else {\n    sz = sizeof(rocblas_double_complex) * lwork;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `cstdlib` for `std::getenv`\n- `rocblas` for `rocblas_double_complex`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1a19a4ff-eead-3484-9f23-15ee456427ea",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that represents the value for the specified environment variable. The problem is that the returned string can be modified by subsequent calls to `std::getenv`, `std::setenv`, or `std::unsetenv`, and this can lead to race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` in multithreaded applications. If you need to use environment variables, consider copying the values into your own buffers to avoid race conditions. Also, always check the returned pointer from `std::getenv` for null before using it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* buffer_size = std::getenv(\"HIPSOLVER_BUFFERSIZE_RETURN_BYTES\");\nif (buffer_size != nullptr) {\n    sz = lwork;\n} else {\n    sz = sizeof(rocblas_double_complex) * lwork;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `cstdlib` for `std::getenv`\n- `rocblas` for `rocblas_double_complex`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
